 {
  "coord": "169:0",
  "verdict": "FAIL",
  "category": "hedge",
  "note": "Source says she 'seemed hardly able to bear' the parting; candidate states it flatly.",
  "replacement": "Napoleon left Dresden on the twenty-ninth of May, after three weeks there among a court of princes, dukes, kings, and even an emperor. Before he went, he rewarded the emperor, kings, and princes who deserved it and scolded those he was unhappy with. To the Empress of Austria he gave pearls and diamonds that were his own — that is, ones he had taken from other kings. And, as his historian tells us, he embraced the Empress Marie Louise tenderly. She regarded him as her husband, though he had left another wife in Paris, and she was left grieving by the parting, which she seemed hardly able to bear. The diplomats still firmly believed peace was possible and worked zealously for it, and Napoleon himself wrote Alexander a letter, addressing him as Monsieur mon frère and sincerely assuring him that he did not want war and would always love and honor him. Even so, he set off to join his army, and at every station he issued fresh orders to hurry his troops along from west to east. He rode in a coach and six, with pages, aides-de-camp, and an escort around him, on the road through Posen, Thorn, Danzig, and Königsberg. In every one of these towns, thousands of people greeted him with excitement and enthusiasm."
 },
